         "step": 1,
         "text": "Let \\( u = x^{2} + 4 \\). Then \\( du = 2 x\\,dx \\), so the integrand is \\( \\frac{3}{2} \\) times \\( \\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\) du: \\( \\frac{3}{2}\\int \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,du \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99002",
         "rule_named": "substitution"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The limits change with the variable: x = 0 gives u = 4 and x = 2 gives u = 8, so the integral is \\( \\frac{3}{2}\\left[\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{4}^{8} \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99003",
         "rule_named": "limits converted to u"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Evaluating gives \\( - \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(4 \\right)}}{2} + \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(8 \\right)}}{2} \\).",
